            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The primary meaning of the sea and waters is primordial chaos, disorder, and cosmic forces of evil hostile to God. A secondary, well-recognized prophetic usage identifies the restless sea/waters as the tumultuous nations and peoples of the earth.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The sea and waters represent peoples and nations, mapped to a cosmic structure: restless and unrooted below the firmament, but still and gathered above as the waters of heaven or the sea of glass.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Primordial chaos and evil, or (in specific prophetic contexts) restless nations.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"Peoples and nations, encompassing both earthly masses and the heavenly sea of glass.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes the concept of primordial chaos entirely and extends the 'nations' referent to the cosmic 'waters above the firmament' and the heavenly 'sea of glass,' which the consensus understands as literal creation, divine purity, or the pacification of chaos, rather than gathered humanity.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The consensus maintains that the waters of Genesis 1 and the general imagery of the sea primarily represent chaos and disorder. The book rejects the chaos referent, universally identifying the waters—even in creation and heavenly architecture—as multitudes of peoples.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By taking a specific prophetic symbol (Revelation 17:15, waters = peoples) and applying it to cosmogonic structures (waters above the firmament) and heavenly realities (the sea of glass), the book diverges from the consensus. It replaces the primary 'chaos' motif with a universalized 'nations' motif.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"PARTIAL\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Reading the 'waters above' in Genesis 1 and the 'sea of glass' in Revelation 4 as human multitudes imports a specific prophetic metaphor into cosmological and architectural texts, ignoring the Ancient Near Eastern context of primordial chaos and the sea as a physical boundary.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
